## Install

```bash
pip install dist/{{ wheel_name }}            # Apple Silicon: pulls mlx-lm for the default encoder
pip install "dist/{{ wheel_name }}[serve]"   # + the HTTP server
```

{% if encoder_backend == "llamacpp" %}This head was trained on **{{ base_model_name }}** (`{{ encoder_model }}`), served by llama.cpp.
Start the embedding server before the first call (see [Encoder](#encoder-must-match-training)).
{% else %}The first call loads the {{ base_model_name }} encoder (`{{ encoder_model }}`, {{ "%.1f" | format(weights_gb) }} GB of weights,
downloaded to the Hugging Face cache on first use unless already cached; afterwards about a second to load).
Resident memory is about the weights plus MLX's free-buffer cache, which the package caps at
{{ cache_limit_mb }} MB (`CLM_MLX_CACHE_LIMIT_MB`).{% endif %}

## Use

```python
from {{ pkg }} import {{ api }}

r = {{ api }}({{ state_example | tojson }})
# {"label": "...", "probabilities": {...}, "confidence": 0.93, "source": "clm", "latency_ms": ...}

# keep your existing logic as the fallback for low-confidence cases
r = {{ api }}(state, fallback=existing_function)          # threshold defaults to {{ "%.2f" | format(threshold) }}
r = {{ api }}(state, threshold=0.7, fallback=existing_function)
```

`confidence` is the System One confidence: top probability minus the mean of the others (0..1).

## Encoder (must match training)

{% if encoder_backend == "llamacpp" %}The head scores **{{ base_model_name }} last-token embeddings** from llama.cpp (`--pooling last`):
tokens without special tokens, tail-truncated to {{ max_len - 1 }} tokens, final hidden state after
the final norm, last token, float32, L2-normalised. It was trained on `{{ encoder_model }}`; run
exactly that GGUF.

```bash
llama-server -m {{ gguf_file }} --embeddings --pooling last -ub {{ max_len }} -c {{ max_len * 4 }} -np 4 --port 8090
CLM_LLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8090 python app.py      # CLM_ENCODER defaults to llamacpp for this package
```

| `CLM_ENCODER` | where | settings |
|---|---|---|
| `llamacpp` (default) | a `llama-server` running the training GGUF | `CLM_LLAMA_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:8090`) |
{% else %}The head scores **{{ base_model_name }} last-token embeddings** ({{ encoder_dim }}-d): tokens without
special tokens, tail-truncated to {{ max_len - 1 }} tokens, final hidden state after the final norm,
last token, float32, L2-normalised. It was trained on the MLX build `{{ encoder_model }}`, which the
package loads by default with no environment variables. Keep that encoder in production, or validate
another build on `examples/golden.jsonl` (or retrain) before switching.

| `CLM_ENCODER` | where | settings |
|---|---|---|
| `mlx` (default) | in-process, Apple Silicon | `CLM_MLX_MODEL` (default `{{ encoder_model }}`, a repo id or local directory), `CLM_MLX_CACHE_LIMIT_MB` (default {{ cache_limit_mb }}) |
| `openai` | any OpenAI-compatible `/v1/embeddings` server serving the same base model, e.g. vLLM on a GPU box | `CLM_EMBED_URL`, `CLM_EMBED_MODEL` (the served name; required), `CLM_EMBED_API_KEY`, `CLM_EMBED_TRUNCATE=0` if the server rejects `truncate_prompt_tokens` |

GPU box example (serve the unquantised original of `{{ encoder_model }}` with last-token pooling):

```bash
vllm serve <original of {{ base_model_name }}> --runner pooling --served-model-name encoder --max-model-len {{ max_len }}
CLM_ENCODER=openai CLM_EMBED_URL=http://gpu-box:8000/v1/embeddings CLM_EMBED_MODEL=encoder python app.py
```
{% endif %}
## HTTP server

```bash
{{ dist }}-serve --port 8700 --warm
curl -s localhost:8700/v1/systemone -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"state": "<the state text>"}'
```

`POST /v1/systemone` speaks the System One wire format (`{state, questions?}` →
`{model, answers, usage}`; `questions` defaults to this package's question). `POST /v1/predict`
returns the same dict as `{{ api }}()`.

